      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The transition from an N-substituted glycosylamine to the Amadori product involves a specific mechanistic pathway that is distinct from the standard glycosylation, often proceeding through an iminium ion intermediate rather than a direct enaminol in the initial step, though the enaminol character is crucial for the subsequent tautomerization.\n\nThe reaction typically initiates with the protonation of the ring oxygen or the nitrogen lone pair to facilitate the departure of the leaving group (e.g., water), generating an oxocarbenium ion. This cationic intermediate is then trapped by the amine nitrogen to form an N-glycoside. However, the rearrangement to the Amadori product (a 1-amino-1-deoxy-2-ketose) requires the migration of the double bond and the shift of the carbonyl group.\n\nThe enaminol intermediate is formed when the nitrogen lone pair donates electron density into the C1-C2 bond, creating a C=N double bond and pushing the C2-OH group into a position to accept a proton. The specific role of the nitrogen lone pair is to act as an internal base or acid depending on the protonation state, stabilizing",
